<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Closing (about 2 minutes, then questions) -->
<section xml:id="sec-closing">
  <title>What I've learned</title>

  <!-- Notes: [Adjust to your experience.] Accessibility is cheapest at authoring time.
       Tools have quirks (PreFigure treats single-letter names like n, s, e, w as compass
       directions). -->
  <slide>
    <title>Lessons from a year of building</title>
    <dl>
      <li><title>The first chapter is the expensive one</title><p>XML, a build pipeline, and figure tools take time to learn; later chapters go much faster.</p></li>
      <li><title>Describe a figure while drawing it</title><p>Writing the description alongside the figure costs minutes; adding it afterwards costs far more.</p></li>
      <li><title>Figures are most of the work</title><p>Diagrams, interactives, and animations take far more time than the prose around them.</p></li>
      <li><title>Notes become a living document</title><p>A fixed typo or a clearer example reaches every student the same day.</p></li>
    </dl>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: Start small: one chapter or one handout. Offer to share the build workflow,
       posting scripts, and tutor, and to sit down with anyone converting a first
       chapter. -->
  <slide>
    <title>Try it on one chapter</title>
    <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" valign="top">
      <program>
<code>
# the command-line tool
pip install pretext

# a new book, built and viewed
pretext new book
pretext build web
pretext view
</code>
      </program>
      <stack>
        <p>Or skip the install: a GitHub Codespace runs the same tools in the browser.</p>
        <p><url href="https://pretextbook.org" visual="pretextbook.org">pretextbook.org</url>: guides, sample books, and community support.</p>
        <p><url href="https://prefigure.org" visual="prefigure.org">prefigure.org</url>: accessible diagrams, inside PreTeXt or on their own.</p>
        <p><alert>Come find me.</alert> I'm glad to share my setup and help convert a first chapter.</p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </slide>

  <!-- Notes: Thank Norman Paris and the Faculty Development AI program. Leave this up for
       questions; Esc shows every slide to jump back to a demo. -->
  <slide>
